WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:02.440
Here's a thought experiment...

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.520
could artificial intelligence
govern us?

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.160
Populism and disinformation are on
the rise, and politics across the

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.520
world seems to be dominated by
emotions and strongman personalities.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.040
Leaders often seem to be more
interested in short-term

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.640
political gains, then the long-term
needs of their electorate.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.000
But could machines do a better job?

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:29.480
Imagine a world where decisions
are made based on impartial

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:30.920
facts and data.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.480
Where the decision makers are
unconcerned by scandals,

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:38.400
immune to corruption and have
no vested interest in maintaining

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:39.600
their popularity.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.400
A world where climate change
is a more pressing issue

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.720
than the results of the
latest focus group.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:49.480
And where global leaders don't risk
instigating World War Three,

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.760
by ranting on Twitter at 02:00AM.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:59.120
In fact, scientists believe there are
no plausible circumstances in which

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.760
machines would or could,
replace governments entirely.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.480
While a machine might be able to make
incredibly complex calculations,

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.480
it would have no objective concept
of right and wrong,

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.160
no definitive way of deciding
what's best.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:17.080
For example, it might be able to
objectively analyse the financial

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.400
cost of keeping someone alive
through medical treatment,

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.320
but it cannot quantify whether
the human life is worth that cost.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.920
And while you could argue our current
politicians may not be subject

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.480
to enough accountability,

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:33.520
it would be impossible to hold a
machine accountable for its mistakes.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.240
After all, what do you do
when a machine misbehaves?

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:38.680
Tell its motherboard?

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:42.880
It's not quite the Terminator,
but perhaps the biggest risk

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.600
in the medium term, is the use of
lethal automated weapons.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:50.240
While there is currently human
oversight, if drones were ever

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.920
authorised to make life or death
decisions, one mistake could trigger

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.720
an automatic reaction
and cause an accidental flash war.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.160
Which frankly sounds a tad more
terrifying than Arnie stealing

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.240
your clothes, boots and motorcycle.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.280
As hard as it might be to believe,
technology which surpasses

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:12.000
human intelligence is decades
if not centuries away.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.560
But even if it existed, scientists
argue that it would be no more useful

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:19.920
in government than the world's
most intelligent human.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.840
Instead, it is far more likely that
the use of artificial intelligence

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.200
in government will continue
on its current trajectory.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.600
As an aid in decision making,
with humans having ultimate power.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:37.680
AI is already being used to assist in
deciding who gets grants or benefits,

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:39.480
in healthcare and policing.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:44.520
But think of it like VAR, with a
human being acting as the referee.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:49.440
Of course as machines are programmed
by humans and their conclusions used

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.480
to support human decisions,
they can be susceptible

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:56.240
to human bias, and their findings
can be used selectively.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.880
Machines learn from data, which is
gathered from the world we live in,

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:02.960
as opposed to the world
we'd like to live in.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.480
In places like the US,

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.520
where African-Americans are often
disproportionately and,

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:12.040
in some cases, lethally targeted
by the police,

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.600
predictive policing could interpret
existing data,

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.160
to potentially perpetuate
those discriminatory patterns.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:24.000
Sadly, it would seem that machine
learning is no more equipped than

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:27.000
human beings to make
big ethical calls.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:31.720
AI would not be an infallible
replacement for flawed human beings.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:34.640
How we use AI to govern,

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:38.640
whether or not it is manipulated
or how mistakes are made,

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:41.520
are all down to human
beings themselves.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.560
In short, AI is much more human
than we ever realised.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:49.880
Which is perhaps
the scariest notion of all.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:57.580
Thanks for watching. Don't forget to subscribe

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:00.600
and click the bell to receive notifications for new videos.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:01.700
See you again soon!

